;; Docs for std/datetime.bjo, read through (:doc #:file "datetime.bjodoc").
;; The texts are samizdat markup: code in prose is @code{...}.

(:doc #:module
  (summary "Dates, times, instants, zones and durations, with the clock as an argument.")
  (see Moment datetime->moment now current-clock date->text text->date)
  (example """
    (import (std datetime))

    (defun (main)
      (def (Some stockholm) (timezone "Europe/Stockholm") :leave-with 1)
      (def (Some noon) (datetime 2026 10 24 #:hours 12) :leave-with 1)
      (def meeting (datetime->moment noon stockholm (resolver GapPush OverlapEarlier)))
      (println (+days meeting 1))     ;; 2026-10-25T12:00:00+01:00[Europe/Stockholm]
      (println (+hours meeting 24))   ;; 2026-10-25T11:00:00+01:00[Europe/Stockholm]
      (println (now))                 ;; 2026-09-28T10:59:20.3067129Z
      0)
    """)
  (reference """
    In the example the clocks go back on the night between, so a calendar day
    later and 24 hours later are two different times.

    One model for time, the one java.time, NodaTime and Racket's Gregor
    share, plus two ideas from OCaml's Ptime: the clock is an argument (with
    a default that a program can rebind), and the types refuse operations
    that mean nothing, such as adding a calendar day to an instant.

    @strong{The types.}

    @read-table{
    | Type                | What it is                                          | Built on |
    |---------------------+-----------------------------------------------------+----------|
    | @code{Date}         | a calendar date, no zone                            | @code{DateOnly} |
    | @code{Time}         | a time of day, no zone                              | @code{TimeOnly} |
    | @code{DateTime}     | a date and a time, no zone                          | @code{DateTime}, Kind always Unspecified |
    | @code{Instant}      | a point on the global timeline                      | a @code{long} of UTC ticks |
    | @code{TimeZone}     | an IANA zone, such as @code{Europe/Stockholm}       | @code{TimeZoneInfo} |
    | @code{Moment}       | a local datetime, its UTC offset, and its zone      | a struct of the three |
    | @code{Duration}     | a fixed length of time: hours, not calendar days    | @code{TimeSpan} |
    | @code{DatePeriod}   | calendar amounts: years, months, weeks, days        | a struct of four @code{int} |
    | @code{Period}       | a date period and a duration: 1 day and 3 hours     | a struct of the two |
    | @code{Clock}        | where @code{now} comes from, and its zone (the prelude's) | a record of functions |
    }

    Each value type is a struct with one field (a @code{Moment} has three),
    so a @code{Date} costs what a @code{DateOnly} costs. No .NET time type
    appears in a public signature.

    @strong{Traits.} Each trait has one method, and everything else is a
    generic function over the traits. A new function, such as a
    @code{->quarter}, never needs a new instance.

    @read-table{
    | Trait             | Method                           | Date | Time | DateTime | Moment | Instant | Duration |
    |-------------------+----------------------------------+------+------+----------+--------+---------+----------|
    | @code{HasDate}    | @code{(->date x)}                | ✓    |      | ✓        | ✓      |         |          |
    | @code{HasTime}    | @code{(->time x)}                |      | ✓    | ✓        | ✓      |         |          |
    | @code{HasInstant} | @code{(->instant x)}             |      |      |          | ✓      | ✓       |          |
    | @code{DateArith}  | @code{(+date-period x p)}        | ✓    |      | ✓        | ✓      |         |          |
    | @code{DateDiff}   | @code{(->date-in-zone-of b a)}   | ✓    |      | ✓        | ✓      |         |          |
    | @code{TimeArith}  | @code{(+duration x d)}           |      | ✓    | ✓        | ✓      | ✓       | ✓        |
    }

    Every type has @code{=} and @code{->str}; all but @code{TimeZone}, the
    periods and the resolvers also have @code{compare}. A @code{TimeZone} is
    equal to another by its id.

    @strong{Construction.} Bad input is @code{None}: @code{(date 2026 2 30)}
    is @code{None}. Nanoseconds are cut to the 100 ns a tick holds:
    @code{(time 1 2 #:nanoseconds 150)} holds 100 ns.

    @strong{Moments.} A local time can be missing from a zone (a gap, when
    the clocks go forward) or happen twice (an overlap, when they go back),
    so @code{datetime->moment} takes a @code{Resolver} saying what to do with
    each, and @code{datetime->moment/opt} answers @code{None} for both.

    Date arithmetic on a moment works on the local time and then finds the
    offset again, since a trait method cannot take a resolver. Through an
    overlap it keeps the moment's own offset if that still fits, and takes
    the earlier one if not. Through a gap it pushes forward. So 2026-10-24
    02:30+02:00 plus a day is 2026-10-25 02:30+02:00, and 2026-10-26
    02:30+01:00 minus a day is 2026-10-25 02:30+01:00.
    @code{+date-period/resolver} and @code{-date-period/resolver} take a
    resolver of their own.

    Duration arithmetic on a moment is exact: through the instant, and back
    into the same zone.

    @strong{The clock.} Only @code{now}, @code{now/moment}, @code{today} and
    @code{clock-timezone} read the time. Each takes @code{#:clock}, which
    defaults to the value of the prelude's parameter @code{current-clock}
    at that call. A clock carries its own zone, which is where @code{today}
    is, and there is no separate current zone. Everything else in the
    module is pure.

    @code{Clock}, @code{make-clock}, @code{system-clock} and
    @code{current-clock} are the prelude's, so @code{(monotonic-ms)} and
    @code{(std stopwatch)} read the same clock as @code{now}. This module
    adds the clocks a test wants: @code{fixed-clock}, @code{manual-clock},
    and @code{clock-in} for a clock of your own in one of its zones.

    @codeblock[#:lang "bjolang"]|{
    (now)                                      ;; the current clock
    (now #:clock some-clock)                   ;; this one
    (today #:tz tokyo)                         ;; the date in Tokyo
    (parameterize ((current-clock test-clock))
      (handle-request req))                    ;; every (now) inside reads test-clock
    }|

    This is the wall clock, and it can step backwards when the machine's
    time is corrected. To measure how long something takes, use the
    prelude's @code{(monotonic-ms)} or @code{(std stopwatch)} instead. They
    read the monotonic part of the same clock, which only moves forward.

    @strong{Arithmetic.} Calendar units (@code{+years}, @code{+months},
    @code{+weeks}, @code{+days} and their @code{-} twins) take an @code{int}
    and work on anything @code{DateArith}; clock units (@code{+hours},
    @code{+minutes}, @code{+seconds}, @code{+milliseconds} and their twins)
    take a @code{long} and work on anything @code{TimeArith}.

    Month arithmetic clamps to the end of the month: 2026-01-31 plus a month
    is 2026-02-28, and 2024-01-31 plus a month is 2024-02-29. A period is
    applied largest unit first: years, then months, then weeks and days,
    then the duration. Time-of-day arithmetic wraps at midnight, modulo 24
    hours, as @code{TimeOnly} and java.time's @code{LocalTime} do. 23:00
    plus two hours is 01:00, 01:00 minus three hours is 22:00, and whole
    days drop out: 12:00 plus @code{(duration #:days 3 #:hours 2)} is 14:00,
    as is 12:00 plus 50 hours. Nothing is refused, so to know how many
    midnights were crossed, add to a @code{DateTime} instead.

    @strong{Weeks.} ISO 8601 weeks, as most of Europe numbers them: a week
    runs Monday to Sunday, and week 1 is the week with the year's first
    Thursday. A week belongs to the year its Thursday is in, so up to three
    days at the start of January can be in the last week of the year
    before, and up to three at the end of December in week 1 of the next:
    2027-01-01 is in week 53 of 2026, and 2025-12-29 in week 1 of 2026. That
    year is @code{->week-year}, and it is the one to print beside
    @code{->week}.

    @strong{Differences.} Whole units, toward zero, from the first argument
    to the second. @code{days-between}, @code{weeks-between},
    @code{months-between} and @code{years-between} read dates only, of two
    values of one type. @code{duration-between} and the
    @code{hours-between} family measure the time that passed, between two
    things with an instant.

    @strong{Text.} @code{->str} writes ISO 8601 for every type, and a reader
    for each reads it back as an @code{Option}:

    @read-table{
    | @code{iso8601->date}        | @code{2026-10-25} |
    | @code{iso8601->time}        | @code{02:30}, @code{02:30:00}, @code{02:30:00.5}, @code{02:30:00,5} |
    | @code{iso8601->datetime}    | @code{2026-10-25T02:30:00} |
    | @code{iso8601->instant}     | @code{2026-10-25T01:30:00Z}, or any offset, converted to UTC |
    | @code{iso8601->moment}      | @code{2026-10-25T02:30:00+01:00[Europe/Stockholm]} |
    | @code{iso8601->duration}    | @code{PT26H30M}, @code{-PT1.5S} |
    | @code{iso8601->date-period} | @code{P1Y2M3W4D}, @code{P1M-3D} |
    | @code{iso8601->period}      | @code{P1DT3H} |
    }

    Only the extended format is read (@code{2026-10-25}, not
    @code{20261025}), with an upper case @code{T} and @code{Z}. Week dates,
    @code{2026-W43-7}, and ordinal dates, @code{2026-298}, are not read. A
    fraction of a second has up to seven digits written, and more are read
    and dropped.

    Text in a format of your own is written by @code{date->text} and its
    neighbours and read by @code{text->date} and its neighbours. The digits
    are written and read by this module, not by .NET, so no culture is
    involved and nothing depends on the machine's locale.

    @strong{Range and errors.} Every value lies between 0001-01-01T00:00 and
    9999-12-31T23:59:59.9999999. A function that returns the value itself
    raises a @code{System.OverflowException} when the result would be
    outside; a constructor, a reader or an @code{/opt} function answers
    @code{None}.

    @codeblock[#:lang "bjolang"]|{
    (-days (date 1 1 1) 1)        ;; raises
    (date 0 12 31)                ;; None
    }|

    Nothing is clamped or wrapped silently, apart from the month-end rule
    and the wrap at midnight above. The checks come before the arithmetic,
    since C# arithmetic wraps where it overflows: @code{(+hours i n)} with an
    @code{n} above about 256 million would multiply past a @code{long} and
    could wrap back into range, so @code{n} is checked first.

    A @code{Duration} holds ±(2@sup{63} − 1) ticks, about ±29 227 years,
    from @code{-PT256204778H48M5.4775807S} to
    @code{PT256204778H48M5.4775807S}. A @code{TimeSpan} goes one tick
    further on the negative side, to a value with no negation; a sum that
    would reach it raises instead, so every duration can be negated, printed
    and read back.

    Near the two ends of the range, a local time and its instant cannot both
    fit: 0001-01-01T00:00 in Tokyo is an instant on the day before, and the
    instant 0001-01-01T00:00Z is a local time on the day before in New York.
    There, @code{datetime->moment} and @code{instant->moment} raise and
    @code{datetime->moment/opt} is @code{None}. This is not a gap, so no
    resolver moves it.

    @strong{Limits.}

    @itemlist{
      @item{@strong{100 ns.} A tick is the finest unit.}
      @item{@strong{Years 1 to 9999}, and the proleptic Gregorian calendar
        throughout.}
      @item{@strong{The zone data is the machine's.} On Linux and macOS,
        .NET reads the IANA database from the operating system
        (@code{/usr/share/zoneinfo} on Linux). A slim container image may
        have none, and then @code{(timezone "Europe/Stockholm")} is
        @code{None}. On Windows, .NET maps an IANA id onto Windows' own zone
        data, which keeps little history, so dates before the 2000s may get
        today's rules (Brussels' 1914 gap is not there).}
      @item{@strong{Offsets are whole minutes.} Before standard time, a zone
        used local mean time, with an offset in seconds: Stockholm's was
        +1:12:12. .NET keeps whole minutes only, so a moment before about
        1900 can be up to a minute off the tz database.}
      @item{@strong{Offsets stop at ±14:00.} .NET caps them there. Nine zones
        had local mean time beyond it: Alaska's before 1867 (Sitka's
        +14:58:47 reads as +14:00), and Manila, Guam, Saipan and Palau
        before 1845 (Manila's −15:56:08 reads as −14:00, so the day it
        skipped is 22 hours and 3 minutes long rather than 24). Those
        moments are off by up to two hours.}
      @item{@strong{No leap seconds}, and no other calendars. Names come in
        English and Swedish, or from a @code{DateNames} of your own.}
    }

    @strong{How a local time finds its offset.} An offset @code{o} fits a
    local time @code{l} when the zone has offset @code{o} at the instant
    @code{l - o}. The candidates are the offsets a day before and a day after
    @code{l}, read as instants. No offset exceeds 16 hours, so those two
    cover every offset in reach, as long as the zone changes its offset at
    most once in 48 hours. Nothing in the tz database comes close: its two
    closest changes, in Freetown in 1939, are 95 hours apart. When a lookup
    answers with a third offset, that becomes a candidate too, which catches
    most layouts with two changes in 48 hours, though not every one.

    How far a change jumps does not matter, only that every offset is under
    a day, so the two lookups land on either side of it. Samoa skipped
    2011-12-30 entirely, going from −10:00 to +14:00, and that day is a gap
    here; Alaska had 1867-10-18 twice, and that day is an overlap. Both are
    in the tests.

    .NET's own @code{IsInvalidTime} and @code{IsAmbiguousTime} are not used.
    They miss the gaps and overlaps that a change of @em{standard} time
    leaves: Brussels went from +00:00 to +01:00 on 1914-11-08, and .NET
    calls 00:30 that night an ordinary time. Here it is a gap.

    @strong{Why it is built this way.} Each of these rules something out,
    and the example is what it rules out. Values are written as ISO text
    here for short.

    @itemlist{
      @item{@strong{An instant is a @code{long} of UTC ticks, not a UTC
        @code{DateTime}.} @code{DateTime.Equals} ignores the Kind, so a
        @code{DateTime} saying 12:00 local time compares equal to 12:00 UTC
        although the two are hours apart. A @code{long} has no Kind to get
        wrong. The cost: the range check on every addition is this
        module's.}
      @item{@strong{The value types are opaque.} Nobody can build a moment
        whose offset its zone never has, such as
        @code{2026-07-01T12:00+01:00[Europe/Stockholm]} (July is +02:00).
        Every moment comes from a function that checked it.}
      @item{@strong{Periods are transparent.} Any mix of numbers is a valid
        period (1 month and 40 days is fine, since months differ in length),
        so there is nothing to protect, and @code{(record-ref p months)}
        works. Equality is field by field:
        @code{(= (date-period #:weeks 1) (date-period #:days 7))} is
        @code{#f}.}
      @item{@strong{Elapsed time is measured between instants only.}
        @code{(hours-between a b)} on two @code{DateTime} values would answer
        the wall-clock difference: 24 hours for 2026-10-25 00:00 to
        2026-10-26 00:00, where 25 passed in Stockholm. Stored local times
        look like timestamps, so that answer would be taken for the time
        that passed, and code generic over anything with a duration between
        could not tell the two apart. So it does not compile, as in
        NodaTime, and the wall-clock reading is written out as a reading in
        UTC. @code{DateTime} is not @code{HasInstant} either, since
        @code{(->instant some-datetime)} would have to guess a zone.}
      @item{@strong{Calendar differences read dates only, and count months as
        java.time does.} @code{(days-between 2026-01-01T23:00
        2026-01-02T01:00)} is 1, although two hours passed;
        @code{hours-between} is for elapsed time.
        @code{(months-between 2026-01-31 2026-02-28)} is 0, although
        @code{(+months 2026-01-31 1)} is 2026-02-28. In return, swapping two
        dates only flips the sign.}
      @item{@strong{Two moments are compared in one zone, the first one's.}
        Read each in its own zone, one instant would be a day apart from
        itself in New York and in Tokyo. Both arguments have one type for
        the same reason: a date against a moment would read the moment's
        local date without saying so, and @code{(->date m)} says it.}
      @item{@strong{@code{->weekday} counts as ISO 8601 does, Monday 1 to
        Sunday 7,} as the text this module reads and writes is ISO 8601. It
        is not called @code{->wday}: C's @code{tm_wday}, Ruby's @code{wday}
        and Racket Gregor's @code{->wday} count Sunday as 0, so
        @code{(= (->wday d) 0)} written from habit would compile and never be
        true. For Sunday as 0, write @code{(% (->weekday d) 7)}.}
      @item{@strong{A week number comes with a year of its own,
        @code{->week-year}.} Printed beside @code{->year}, 2027-01-01 would
        be week 53 of 2027, a week 2027 does not have, and 2025-12-29 week 1
        of 2025, a year early. The two are separate functions rather than
        one pair, since asking which week it is usually wants the number
        only; the two years differ in at most three days at either end of a
        year.}
      @item{@strong{A format is a quoted list, not a pattern string.} In a
        pattern string a letter's case changes its meaning: Java's
        @code{YYYY} is the week-based year where @code{yyyy} is the year, so
        @code{YYYY-MM-dd} writes 2025-12-29 as 2026-12-29, and @code{mm} is
        minutes where @code{MM} is months. Here a field has a name, a wrong
        name is a compile error that lists the right ones, and text needs no
        escaping, being a string of its own. The cost: a format is longer
        than @code{yyyy-MM-dd}, and cannot arrive as text from a
        configuration file; a program chooses among formats it holds.}
      @item{@strong{One format union and one function per type.} That is what
        makes @code{hours} in a date's format a compile error. A moment's
        date is written with @code{(date->text (->date m) ...)}, which says
        whose date it is, as a calendar difference asks for @code{->date}
        too.}
      @item{@strong{A format that could read a text two ways is refused, not
        read greedily.} With @code{'(year month day)}, January 11 and
        November 1 of 2026 are both written @code{2026111}, so no reader can
        tell which was meant. Read greedily, @code{2026110}, which the same
        format writes for January 10, would be November 0, and
        @code{None}. Trying every split instead would still have to pick one
        of the two dates. So the format is refused when it is used to read,
        and the message says what reads one way: a width, or text between
        the parts. The cost: a format that is fine for the dates a program
        has, such as @code{'(year month day)} for dates whose month and day
        both have two digits, is refused anyway.}
      @item{@strong{A field left out is its first value, rather than
        refused.} HTML's month input sends @code{2026-10} and its week input
        @code{2026-W43}, and a text without a day means the month itself;
        reading it as the 1st, and a week as its Monday, gives a
        @code{Date} that stands for it with no second function. The cost:
        that @code{Date} is the month's first day, not a month, and written
        back with a format that has a day it says @code{01}.}
      @item{@strong{Month, day and @code{yday} count within @code{year}, and
        @code{week} within @code{week-year}.} The two years differ in up to
        three days at each end of a year, so a format that mixes them up is
        right most of the year and wrong near New Year, as Java's
        @code{YYYY-MM-dd} is. Read with @code{'((year 4) "-W" (week 2))},
        @code{2026-W01} would be January 1, the Thursday of week 1, and
        @code{2026-W43} nothing. Such a format is refused whatever the text,
        so the mix-up shows the first time the format is used.}
      @item{@strong{@code{text->moment} never guesses a zone.} RFC 3339's
        @code{2026-10-25T02:30:00+01:00} has an offset, and +01:00 is
        Stockholm's, Paris's, Lagos's and a dozen others'; a day later the
        first two are still at +01:00 and Lagos too, but in March they part.
        A moment read in a guessed zone would do its calendar arithmetic by
        the wrong rules, a month later and silently. So a moment's format
        needs @code{zone}, and a text without one is an instant, which
        @code{instant->moment} puts in a zone the program names. A text's
        offset is not a zone either: it is checked against the zone read, as
        @code{iso8601->moment} checks it.}
      @item{@strong{An instant's fields are UTC's unless its format has an
        offset.} That is what @code{instant->text} writes, so the two are
        inverses, and it is the one reading that needs no zone: a program
        that stores UTC text writes and reads it with one format. A text in
        some local time without an offset is a @code{DateTime}, and becomes
        an instant only through a zone the program names, with
        @code{datetime->moment}.}
      @item{@strong{A moment's @code{=} compares every field, and
        @code{compare} orders by instant, then local time, then zone id,} so
        the two agree. 12:00+02:00[Europe/Stockholm] and 10:00+00:00[UTC]
        are one instant and two values; a sorted set keeps both, side by
        side. For the same instant, compare @code{(->instant a)} and
        @code{(->instant b)}.}
      @item{@strong{The resolvers are a closed set.} A resolver of your own
        (on a gap, go back to the last valid time) needs a new case here. In
        return, no resolver can produce a moment at a local time or in a
        zone that was not asked for, and @code{/opt} covers refusing.}
      @item{@strong{Leaving the range raises.}
        @code{(+days (date 9999 12 31) 1)} raises rather than answering an
        @code{Option}, which would force a @code{match} after every
        addition. Constructors and readers answer @code{None}, since bad
        input is expected there.}
      @item{@strong{Calendar units take @code{int}, clock units take
        @code{long}.} No date is two billion days away, but an @code{int} of
        seconds runs out at 68 years.}
      @item{@strong{@code{now/moment} answers a @code{Moment}.} An
        @code{Instant} has no zone, so now in Stockholm cannot be one.}
      @item{@strong{The clock is a keyword argument, defaulting to a
        parameter read at each call.} A test binds @code{current-clock}
        around code it did not write:
        @code{(parameterize ((current-clock (fixed-clock t z))) (render-invoice order))}
        makes every @code{(now)} and @code{(today)} inside answer @code{t},
        however deep, with no clock passed down through every function
        between. A binding lasts only inside its @code{parameterize} and the
        fibers started there, so one test cannot leave a fake clock behind
        for the next, as it could with a global clock set and reset. It is a
        parameter rather than an effect by the prelude's rule: a clock is a
        value the caller reads, as a random source is. The cost: a function
        that calls @code{(now)} without @code{#:clock} does not say in its
        signature that it reads the time.}
      @item{@strong{The zone travels with the clock.} There is no
        current-zone parameter, so a test that fixes the clock fixes
        @code{today} too: under a fixed clock in Stockholm, @code{(today)} is
        Stockholm's date, whether the test runs in Stockholm or on a build
        machine set to UTC. With a zone parameter of its own, a test could
        fix the instant and still get the machine's date. @code{#:tz} asks
        about another zone, so there are no @code{/zone} twins.}
      @item{@strong{A manual clock is a @code{Clock}, not a type of its
        own.} So @code{clock-advance!} on a fixed clock is an error when it
        runs rather than when it compiles. In return, a manual clock goes
        wherever a clock does, @code{current-clock} and @code{#:clock}
        included, with no conversion.}
      @item{@strong{One clock, two readings.} A @code{Clock} answers both
        @code{now} and the prelude's @code{monotonic-ms}. The monotonic
        reading counts milliseconds from an unspecified start, has no time
        of day, and never steps back, which is what measuring an interval
        needs. Binding @code{current-clock} to a fake moves both, so a test
        cannot fix one and leave the other running.}
      @item{@strong{A clock is a record of functions.} Anyone can make one
        with the prelude's @code{make-clock}; @code{fixed-clock} and
        @code{manual-clock} are made that way. @code{clock-advance!} calls
        the clock's own @code{advance-ticks}, which is how a manual clock
        moves.}
      @item{@strong{@code{DateTime} is this module's.} @code{(std clr-ord)}
        orders @code{System.DateTime} and the other .NET time types without
        exporting a name for them, so a module that imports both has one
        @code{DateTime}, and code at the edge to .NET writes
        @code{System.DateTime}. Renaming this one @code{LocalDateTime}, as
        java.time and NodaTime do, would have asked for @code{LocalDate} and
        @code{LocalTime} beside it.}
    }

    @strong{Also exported.} @code{date+date-period},
    @code{datetime+date-period}, @code{moment+date-period},
    @code{datetime+duration}, @code{instant+duration},
    @code{moment+duration} and @code{duration+duration} are the trait
    methods at one type. An importing module copies the trait methods'
    bodies into its own code, and those bodies call these, so the compiler
    exports them too.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Types
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc Date
  (summary "A calendar date, with no time and no zone.")
  (see date iso8601->date HasDate)
  (reference """
    Built on @code{DateOnly}, and costs what one costs. Opaque: every date
    comes from a function that checked it.
    """))

(:doc Time
  (summary "A time of day, with no date and no zone.")
  (see time iso8601->time HasTime)
  (reference """
    Built on @code{TimeOnly}, at a precision of 100 ns. Arithmetic on a time
    wraps at midnight.
    """))

(:doc DateTime
  (summary "A date and a time of day, with no zone.")
  (see datetime date+time->datetime datetime->moment)
  (reference """
    Built on a @code{System.DateTime} whose Kind is always Unspecified. It
    has no instant, so it is not @code{HasInstant}: put it in a zone with
    @code{datetime->moment} first. The name is this module's; code at the
    edge to .NET writes @code{System.DateTime}.
    """))

(:doc Instant
  (summary "A point on the global timeline, with no zone.")
  (see now instant->moment HasInstant)
  (reference """
    A @code{long} of UTC ticks rather than a UTC @code{DateTime}, whose
    equality ignores the Kind. @code{->str} writes it in UTC, with a
    @code{Z}.
    """))

(:doc TimeZone
  (summary "An IANA time zone, such as Europe/Stockholm.")
  (see timezone utc timezone-id)
  (reference """
    Built on @code{TimeZoneInfo}. Two zones are equal when their ids are,
    and @code{->str} writes the id. A zone has no @code{compare}.
    """))

(:doc Moment
  (summary "A local date and time, its UTC offset, and its zone.")
  (see datetime->moment instant->moment iso8601->moment)
  (reference """
    Opaque, so its offset is always one its zone has at that local time:
    nobody can build @code{2026-07-01T12:00+01:00[Europe/Stockholm]} (July is
    +02:00).

    @code{=} compares every field, so 12:00+02:00[Europe/Stockholm] and
    10:00+00:00[UTC] are one instant and two values. @code{compare} orders
    by instant, then local time, then zone id, so that it is 0 exactly when
    @code{=} holds. For the same instant, compare @code{(->instant a)} and
    @code{(->instant b)}.
    """))

(:doc Duration
  (summary "A fixed length of time: hours, minutes and seconds, not calendar days.")
  (see duration iso8601->duration duration-between)
  (reference """
    Built on @code{TimeSpan}, and holds ±(2@sup{63} − 1) ticks, about
    ±29 227 years. @code{(duration #:days 1)} is exactly 24 hours; a
    calendar day is a @code{DatePeriod}.
    """))

(:doc DatePeriod
  (summary "Calendar amounts: years, months, weeks and days.")
  (field years "Years.")
  (field months "Months.")
  (field weeks "Weeks.")
  (field days "Days.")
  (see date-period +date-period iso8601->date-period)
  (reference """
    Transparent, since any mix of numbers is a valid period: 1 month and 40
    days is fine, as months differ in length. Equality is field by field, so
    @code{(= (date-period #:weeks 1) (date-period #:days 7))} is @code{#f}.
    It is applied largest unit first: years, then months, then weeks and
    days together.
    """))

(:doc Period
  (summary "A date period and a duration: 1 day and 3 hours.")
  (field calendar "The calendar part, a DatePeriod, applied first.")
  (field exact "The exact part, a Duration, applied after the calendar part.")
  (see period +period iso8601->period))

(:doc Gap
  (summary "What datetime->moment does with a local time the zone skips.")
  (case GapPush "Forward by the length of the gap: 02:30 in a one-hour gap is 03:30.")
  (case GapNextValid "The first time after the gap: 03:00.")
  (see resolver Overlap)
  (reference """
    In Stockholm, 2026-03-29 02:30 never happens: @code{GapPush} gives
    03:30+02:00 and @code{GapNextValid} 03:00+02:00.
    """))

(:doc Overlap
  (summary "What datetime->moment does with a local time the zone has twice.")
  (case OverlapEarlier "The first of the two, at the larger offset.")
  (case OverlapLater "The second.")
  (see resolver Gap)
  (reference """
    In Stockholm, 2026-10-25 02:30 happens twice: @code{OverlapEarlier}
    gives +02:00 (00:30Z) and @code{OverlapLater} +01:00 (01:30Z).
    """))

(:doc Resolver
  (summary "What to do with a local time in a gap and with one in an overlap.")
  (field gap "What to do in a gap.")
  (field overlap "What to do in an overlap.")
  (see resolver datetime->moment)
  (reference """
    A closed set: a resolver of your own, such as going back to the last
    valid time on a gap, would need a new case here. In return, no resolver
    can produce a moment at a local time or in a zone that was not asked
    for, and @code{datetime->moment/opt} covers refusing.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Traits
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc HasDate
  (summary "Has a calendar date: Date, DateTime and Moment.")
  (tparam %a "The type with a date.")
  (see ->year ->month ->day ->weekday ->week))

(:doc ->date
  (summary "The date of a value; a moment's local date.")
  (arg x "A date, a datetime or a moment.")
  (returns "Its date."))

(:doc HasTime
  (summary "Has a time of day: Time, DateTime and Moment.")
  (tparam %a "The type with a time of day.")
  (see ->hours ->minutes ->seconds ->nanoseconds))

(:doc ->time
  (summary "The time of day of a value; a moment's local time.")
  (arg x "A time, a datetime or a moment.")
  (returns "Its time of day."))

(:doc HasInstant
  (summary "Is a point on the timeline: Instant and Moment.")
  (tparam %a "The type with an instant.")
  (see duration-between ->unix)
  (reference """
    @code{DateTime} is not one, since @code{(->instant some-datetime)} would
    have to guess a zone.
    """))

(:doc ->instant
  (summary "The instant of a value.")
  (arg x "An instant or a moment.")
  (returns "Its instant; a moment's is its local time less its offset."))

(:doc DateArith
  (summary "Takes calendar arithmetic: Date, DateTime and Moment.")
  (tparam %a "The type that takes a DatePeriod.")
  (see +days +months -date-period +period))

(:doc +date-period
  (summary "A value moved by calendar amounts.")
  (arg x "A date, a datetime or a moment.")
  (arg p "The period, applied years first, then months, then weeks and days.")
  (returns "x moved by p, of x's type.")
  (raises OverflowException "When the result is outside the calendar.")
  (see -date-period +date-period/resolver)
  (reference """
    Month arithmetic clamps to the end of the month: 2026-01-31 plus a month
    is 2026-02-28. On a moment the local time moves and the offset is found
    again: through an overlap it keeps the moment's own offset if that still
    fits, and takes the earlier one if not; through a gap it pushes forward.
    """))

(:doc DateDiff
  (summary "Takes calendar differences: Date, DateTime and Moment.")
  (tparam %a "The type whose dates are compared.")
  (see days-between months-between))

(:doc ->date-in-zone-of
  (summary "The date of b where a is, which calendar differences compare with a's own date.")
  (arg b "The value whose date is wanted.")
  (arg a "The value whose zone it is read in.")
  (returns "b's date; for two moments, b's date in a's zone.")
  (raises OverflowException "Near either end of the calendar, when b moved into a's zone is outside it.")
  (reference """
    A moment @code{b} is first moved into @code{a}'s zone, so two moments of
    one instant are the same day wherever they are. A date or a datetime is
    its own date.
    """))

(:doc TimeArith
  (summary "Takes a Duration: Time, DateTime, Moment, Instant and Duration.")
  (tparam %a "The type that takes a Duration.")
  (see +hours -duration +period))

(:doc +duration
  (summary "A value moved by an exact length of time.")
  (arg x "A time, a datetime, a moment, an instant or a duration.")
  (arg d "The duration.")
  (returns "x moved by d, of x's type.")
  (raises OverflowException "When the result is outside the calendar, or a duration outside ±29 227 years.")
  (see -duration)
  (reference """
    A time wraps at midnight and never raises. A moment moves through its
    instant and is put back in the same zone, so it is exact. A duration
    plus a duration is their sum: @code{(+hours (duration #:minutes 30) 1)}
    is PT1H30M.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Construction
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc date
  (summary "A date from a year, a month and a day, or None if there is no such date.")
  (arg y "The year, 1 to 9999.")
  (arg m "The month, 1 to 12.")
  (arg d "The day of the month.")
  (returns "Some date, or None: @code{(date 2026 2 30)} is None.")
  (see week-date iso8601->date))

(:doc time
  (summary "A time of day, or None if there is no such time.")
  (arg h "The hour, 0 to 23.")
  (arg m "The minute, 0 to 59.")
  (key #:seconds "The second, 0 to 59.")
  (key #:nanoseconds "The fraction of the second, 0 to 999 999 999, cut to the 100 ns a tick holds.")
  (returns "Some time, or None.")
  (see datetime iso8601->time)
  (example """
    (time 13 5)                     ;; (Some 13:05:00)
    (time 1 2 #:nanoseconds 150)    ;; holds 100 ns
    """))

(:doc datetime
  (summary "A date and a time of day, or None if there is no such datetime.")
  (arg y "The year, 1 to 9999.")
  (arg mo "The month, 1 to 12.")
  (arg d "The day of the month.")
  (key #:hours "The hour, 0 to 23.")
  (key #:minutes "The minute, 0 to 59.")
  (key #:seconds "The second, 0 to 59.")
  (key #:nanoseconds "The fraction of the second, cut to the 100 ns a tick holds.")
  (returns "Some datetime, or None.")
  (see date+time->datetime iso8601->datetime)
  (example """
    (datetime 2026 10 24 #:hours 12)   ;; (Some 2026-10-24T12:00:00)
    """))

(:doc date+time->datetime
  (summary "A datetime from a date and a time.")
  (arg d "The date.")
  (arg t "The time of day.")
  (returns "The datetime.")
  (see ->datetime))

(:doc ->datetime
  (summary "The date and time of anything with both, as a DateTime.")
  (arg x "A datetime or a moment.")
  (returns "Its date and time; a moment's local ones.")
  (see date+time->datetime datetime->text)
  (reference """
    How a moment is written with a datetime's format:
    @code{(datetime->text (->datetime some-moment) ...)}.
    """))

(:doc duration
  (summary "A fixed length of time, the sum of the units given.")
  (key #:days "Days of exactly 24 hours.")
  (key #:hours "Hours.")
  (key #:minutes "Minutes.")
  (key #:seconds "Seconds.")
  (key #:milliseconds "Milliseconds.")
  (key #:nanoseconds "Nanoseconds, cut to the 100 ns a tick holds.")
  (returns "The duration.")
  (raises OverflowException "When a unit or the sum is outside ±29 227 years.")
  (see date-period period)
  (example """
    (duration #:hours 26 #:minutes 30)   ;; PT26H30M
    """)
  (reference """
    A day here is exactly 24 hours. For calendar days, which can be 23 or 25
    hours long across a change of offset, use a @code{DatePeriod}.
    """))

(:doc date-period
  (summary "Calendar amounts: years, months, weeks and days.")
  (key #:years "Years.")
  (key #:months "Months.")
  (key #:weeks "Weeks.")
  (key #:days "Days.")
  (returns "The period. Any mix of amounts, negative ones included, is a period.")
  (see period duration +date-period)
  (example """
    (date-period #:months 1 #:days -3)   ;; P1M-3D
    """))

(:doc period
  (summary "A date period and a duration together: 1 day and 3 hours.")
  (key #:years "Years.")
  (key #:months "Months.")
  (key #:weeks "Weeks.")
  (key #:days "Calendar days.")
  (key #:hours "Hours.")
  (key #:minutes "Minutes.")
  (key #:seconds "Seconds.")
  (key #:milliseconds "Milliseconds.")
  (key #:nanoseconds "Nanoseconds, cut to the 100 ns a tick holds.")
  (returns "The period.")
  (raises OverflowException "When the clock units are outside ±29 227 years.")
  (see +period date-period duration)
  (example """
    (period #:days 1 #:hours 3)   ;; P1DT3H
    """)
  (reference """
    @code{#:days} is a calendar day here, in the date part; the clock units
    make the duration.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Zones and moments
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc timezone
  (summary "The IANA zone with this id, or None.")
  (arg id "An IANA id, such as @code{Europe/Stockholm}.")
  (returns "Some zone, or None for an id the machine does not know.")
  (see utc timezone-id)
  (example """
    (def (Some stockholm) (timezone "Europe/Stockholm") :leave-with 1)
    """)
  (reference """
    IANA ids only, so a Windows id such as @code{"W. Europe Standard Time"}
    is @code{None}, and so is every id on a machine without zone data (see
    the module's limits). Stop there rather than fall back to @code{utc}:
    @code{(option-value (timezone "Europe/Stockholm") utc)} would put every
    date and time in UTC without a word, on exactly the machines that lack
    the data.
    """))

(:doc utc
  (summary "The UTC zone, which every machine has.")
  (see timezone))

(:doc timezone-id
  (summary "A zone's IANA id.")
  (arg z "The zone.")
  (returns "Its id, such as @code{Europe/Stockholm}; what @code{->str} writes."))

(:doc timezone-offset-at
  (summary "A zone's UTC offset at an instant.")
  (arg z "The zone.")
  (arg i "The instant.")
  (returns "The offset, as a Duration: PT2H in Stockholm in summer.")
  (see ->utc-offset instant->moment))

(:doc resolver
  (summary "A resolver from what to do in a gap and in an overlap.")
  (arg g "GapPush or GapNextValid.")
  (arg o "OverlapEarlier or OverlapLater.")
  (returns "The resolver.")
  (see datetime->moment Gap Overlap)
  (example """
    (resolver GapPush OverlapEarlier)
    """)
  (reference """
    @read-table{
    | @code{GapPush}        | forward by the length of the gap: 02:30 in a one-hour gap is 03:30 |
    | @code{GapNextValid}   | the first time after the gap: 03:00 |
    | @code{OverlapEarlier} | the first of the two, at the larger offset |
    | @code{OverlapLater}   | the second |
    }

    In Stockholm, 2026-10-25 02:30 happens twice: @code{OverlapEarlier}
    gives +02:00 (00:30Z) and @code{OverlapLater} +01:00 (01:30Z).
    2026-03-29 02:30 never happens: @code{GapPush} gives 03:30+02:00 and
    @code{GapNextValid} 03:00+02:00.
    """))

(:doc datetime->moment
  (summary "A local time in a zone, with a resolver for a gap or an overlap.")
  (arg dt "The local time.")
  (arg z "The zone.")
  (arg r "What to do when dt is in a gap or an overlap.")
  (returns "The moment. Always one, since the resolver decides.")
  (raises OverflowException "Near either end of the calendar, when the local time and its instant cannot both fit.")
  (see datetime->moment/opt resolver instant->moment)
  (example """
    (datetime->moment noon stockholm (resolver GapPush OverlapEarlier))
    """)
  (reference """
    A local time can be missing from a zone (a gap, when the clocks go
    forward) or happen twice (an overlap, when they go back); the resolver
    says what to do with each. 0001-01-01T00:00 in Tokyo is an instant on the
    day before: that is not a gap, so no resolver moves it, and this raises.
    """))

(:doc datetime->moment/opt
  (summary "A local time in a zone, or None in a gap or an overlap.")
  (arg dt "The local time.")
  (arg z "The zone.")
  (returns "Some moment when the local time happens exactly once in z, or None. None too when its instant is outside the calendar.")
  (see datetime->moment))

(:doc instant->moment
  (summary "The moment an instant is in a zone.")
  (arg i "The instant.")
  (arg z "The zone.")
  (returns "The moment. Always one answer.")
  (raises OverflowException "Near either end of the calendar, when the local time is outside it: the instant 0001-01-01T00:00Z in New York.")
  (see adjust-timezone datetime->moment))

(:doc +date-period/resolver
  (summary "A moment moved by calendar amounts, with a resolver for where it lands.")
  (arg m "The moment.")
  (arg p "The period.")
  (arg r "What to do when the new local time is in a gap or an overlap.")
  (returns "The moment in m's zone.")
  (raises OverflowException "When the result is outside the calendar.")
  (see -date-period/resolver +date-period)
  (reference """
    @code{+date-period} on a moment keeps its offset through an overlap if
    that still fits, and pushes forward through a gap; this says otherwise.
    """))

(:doc -date-period/resolver
  (summary "A moment moved back by calendar amounts, with a resolver for where it lands.")
  (arg m "The moment.")
  (arg p "The period, subtracted.")
  (arg r "What to do when the new local time is in a gap or an overlap.")
  (returns "The moment in m's zone.")
  (raises OverflowException "When the result is outside the calendar.")
  (see +date-period/resolver -date-period))

(:doc ->utc-offset
  (summary "A moment's UTC offset.")
  (arg m "The moment.")
  (returns "Its offset, as a Duration.")
  (see timezone-offset-at))

(:doc ->timezone
  (summary "A moment's zone.")
  (arg m "The moment.")
  (returns "Its zone."))

(:doc adjust-timezone
  (summary "The same instant in another zone.")
  (arg m "The moment.")
  (arg z "The zone to put it in.")
  (returns "A moment at m's instant in z.")
  (raises OverflowException "Near either end of the calendar, when the local time in z is outside it.")
  (see instant->moment))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Instants
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc instant->utc-datetime
  (summary "An instant's date and time in UTC.")
  (arg i "The instant.")
  (returns "Its UTC date and time, as a DateTime.")
  (see utc-datetime->instant))

(:doc utc-datetime->instant
  (summary "The instant of a date and time read as UTC.")
  (arg dt "A date and time in UTC.")
  (returns "The instant.")
  (see instant->utc-datetime datetime->moment)
  (reference """
    It takes any @code{DateTime}, so a local time goes in and is read as UTC
    without a word: put a local time in its zone with
    @code{datetime->moment}. Read on purpose, it spells out a wall-clock
    difference: @code{(hours-between (utc-datetime->instant a)
    (utc-datetime->instant b))}.
    """))

(:doc instant->unix-seconds
  (summary "An instant's Unix time in seconds, rounded toward the past.")
  (arg i "The instant.")
  (returns "Seconds since 1970-01-01T00:00Z. Half a second before 1970 is -1.")
  (see unix-seconds->instant ->unix))

(:doc unix-seconds->instant
  (summary "The instant of a Unix time in seconds.")
  (arg s "Seconds since 1970-01-01T00:00Z.")
  (returns "Some instant, or None outside the years 1 to 9999.")
  (see instant->unix-seconds unix-milliseconds->instant))

(:doc instant->unix-milliseconds
  (summary "An instant's Unix time in milliseconds, rounded toward the past.")
  (arg i "The instant.")
  (returns "Milliseconds since 1970-01-01T00:00Z. Half a millisecond before 1970 is -1.")
  (see unix-milliseconds->instant))

(:doc unix-milliseconds->instant
  (summary "The instant of a Unix time in milliseconds.")
  (arg ms "Milliseconds since 1970-01-01T00:00Z.")
  (returns "Some instant, or None outside the years 1 to 9999.")
  (see instant->unix-milliseconds unix-seconds->instant))

(:doc ->unix
  (summary "The Unix time in seconds of an instant or a moment.")
  (arg x "An instant or a moment.")
  (returns "Its instant's Unix time, rounded toward the past, whatever a moment's zone.")
  (see instant->unix-seconds))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; The clock
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc clock-in
  (summary "A clock, with today in another zone.")
  (arg c "The clock to read.")
  (arg z "The zone today is to be in.")
  (returns "A clock reading what c reads, with z as its zone.")
  (see system-clock-in clock-timezone)
  (example """
    (clock-in (make-clock #:utc-ticks recorded-ticks) stockholm)
    """)
  (reference """
    The prelude's @code{Clock} carries a .NET @code{TimeZoneInfo}; this is
    how a clock of your own gets one of this module's zones.
    """))

(:doc system-clock-in
  (summary "The machine's clock, in a zone of your choosing.")
  (arg z "The zone today is to be in.")
  (returns "A clock reading the system time, with z as its zone.")
  (see clock-in now)
  (example """
    (defun (main)
      (def (Some stockholm) (timezone "Europe/Stockholm") :leave-with 1)
      (parameterize ((current-clock (system-clock-in stockholm)))
        (serve)))
    """)
  (reference """
    A machine set to UTC, as most containers are, would otherwise make
    @code{(today)} UTC's date, which in Stockholm is still yesterday until
    01:00, or 02:00 in summer. A program binds it once, around everything it
    runs, and the fibers it starts inherit it.
    """))

(:doc fixed-clock
  (summary "A clock that always answers the same instant.")
  (arg i "The instant it answers.")
  (arg z "Its zone.")
  (returns "The clock. Its monotonic-ms stands still too.")
  (see manual-clock now))

(:doc manual-clock
  (summary "A clock that stands still until clock-advance! moves it.")
  (arg i "The instant it starts at.")
  (arg z "Its zone.")
  (returns "The clock.")
  (see clock-advance! fixed-clock)
  (reference """
    The clock takes a lock to advance and to answer, so fibers on several
    threads may share one: every advance counts, and the next read sees it.

    Its @code{monotonic-ms} starts at 0 and moves with every forward
    advance. A negative advance steps only the wall clock back, so a
    stopwatch on it never reads a negative time.
    """))

(:doc clock-advance!
  (summary "Moves a clock that can be moved, as a manual clock can.")
  (arg c "A clock with an advance: one from manual-clock, or made with #:advance.")
  (arg d "How far. A negative duration steps it back, as a corrected wall clock does.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When the clock has no advance, as the system clock and a fixed one have not.")
  (raises OverflowException "When the new instant is outside the calendar.")
  (see manual-clock)
  (reference """
    Calls the clock's @code{advance-ticks} with the duration in ticks. A
    manual clock is a @code{Clock}, not a type of its own, so this on
    another clock is an error when it runs rather than when it compiles.
    """))

(:doc clock-timezone
  (summary "A clock's zone.")
  (key #:clock "The clock.")
  (returns "Its zone: the machine's for system-clock.")
  (see today))

(:doc now
  (summary "The current instant.")
  (key #:clock "The clock to read.")
  (returns "What the clock says now.")
  (see now/moment today)
  (example """
    (now)                     ;; the current clock
    (now #:clock some-clock)  ;; this one
    """))

(:doc now/moment
  (summary "The current moment, in the clock's zone or another.")
  (key #:clock "The clock to read.")
  (key #:tz "The zone to put it in.")
  (returns "What the clock says now, as a moment.")
  (see now today)
  (reference """
    A @code{Moment} rather than an @code{Instant}, since an instant has no
    zone and so now in Stockholm cannot be one.
    """))

(:doc today
  (summary "Today's date, in the clock's zone or another.")
  (key #:clock "The clock to read.")
  (key #:tz "The zone whose date it is.")
  (returns "The date there now.")
  (see now/moment system-clock-in)
  (example """
    (today #:tz tokyo)   ;; the date in Tokyo
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Accessors
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc ->year
  (summary "The year of anything with a date.")
  (arg x "A date, a datetime or a moment; a moment's local date.")
  (returns "The year, 1 to 9999.")
  (see ->week-year))

(:doc ->month
  (summary "The month of anything with a date.")
  (arg x "A date, a datetime or a moment; a moment's local date.")
  (returns "The month, 1 to 12."))

(:doc ->day
  (summary "The day of the month of anything with a date.")
  (arg x "A date, a datetime or a moment; a moment's local date.")
  (returns "The day, 1 to 31."))

(:doc ->weekday
  (summary "The day of the week, Monday 1 to Sunday 7, as ISO 8601 counts.")
  (arg x "A date, a datetime or a moment; a moment's local date.")
  (returns "1 for Monday up to 7 for Sunday.")
  (reference """
    It is not called @code{->wday}, since C's @code{tm_wday}, Ruby's
    @code{wday} and Racket Gregor's @code{->wday} count Sunday as 0. For
    Sunday as 0, write @code{(% (->weekday d) 7)}.
    """))

(:doc ->yday
  (summary "The day of the year of anything with a date.")
  (arg x "A date, a datetime or a moment; a moment's local date.")
  (returns "The day, 1 to 366."))

(:doc ->week
  (summary "The ISO 8601 week number of anything with a date.")
  (arg x "A date, a datetime or a moment; a moment's local date.")
  (returns "The week, 1 to 53, of the year ->week-year answers.")
  (see ->week-year weeks-in-year week-date)
  (reference """
    A week runs Monday to Sunday and belongs to the year its Thursday is in,
    so 2027-01-01 is in week 53 of 2026. Print it beside
    @code{->week-year}, not @code{->year}.
    """))

(:doc ->week-year
  (summary "The year ->week counts in.")
  (arg x "A date, a datetime or a moment; a moment's local date.")
  (returns "The year of the date's ISO week: 2026 for 2027-01-01, and 2026 for 2025-12-29.")
  (see ->week ->year)
  (reference """
    It differs from @code{->year} in at most three days at either end of a
    year. The two are separate functions, since asking which week it is
    usually wants the number only.
    """))

(:doc weeks-in-year
  (summary "How many ISO weeks a week-year has.")
  (arg y "The week-year.")
  (returns "52 or 53.")
  (raises OverflowException "When y is outside 1 to 9999.")
  (see week-date ->week))

(:doc week-date
  (summary "The date of a day of an ISO week, or None.")
  (arg y "The week-year.")
  (arg w "The week, 1 to 53.")
  (arg d "The day of the week, 1 for Monday to 7 for Sunday.")
  (returns "Some date, or None for a week the year does not have, such as week 53 of 2027.")
  (see ->week ->week-year weeks-in-year)
  (example """
    (week-date 2026 43 1)   ;; (Some 2026-10-19)
    """))

(:doc ->hours
  (summary "The hour of anything with a time of day.")
  (arg x "A time, a datetime or a moment; a moment's local time.")
  (returns "The hour, 0 to 23."))

(:doc ->minutes
  (summary "The minute of anything with a time of day.")
  (arg x "A time, a datetime or a moment; a moment's local time.")
  (returns "The minute, 0 to 59."))

(:doc ->seconds
  (summary "The second of anything with a time of day.")
  (arg x "A time, a datetime or a moment; a moment's local time.")
  (returns "The second, 0 to 59."))

(:doc ->milliseconds
  (summary "The millisecond of anything with a time of day.")
  (arg x "A time, a datetime or a moment; a moment's local time.")
  (returns "The whole milliseconds of the second, 0 to 999."))

(:doc ->nanoseconds
  (summary "The whole fraction of the second, in nanoseconds.")
  (arg x "A time, a datetime or a moment; a moment's local time.")
  (returns "0 to 999 999 900, always a multiple of 100."))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Arithmetic
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc +years
  (summary "Years later.")
  (arg x "A date, a datetime or a moment.")
  (arg n "How many years.")
  (returns "x moved by n years; February 29 becomes February 28 in a year without one.")
  (raises OverflowException "When the result is outside the calendar.")
  (see -years +date-period))

(:doc +months
  (summary "Months later, clamped to the end of the month.")
  (arg x "A date, a datetime or a moment.")
  (arg n "How many months.")
  (returns "x moved by n months: 2026-01-31 plus a month is 2026-02-28, and 2024-01-31 plus a month 2024-02-29.")
  (raises OverflowException "When the result is outside the calendar.")
  (see -months months-between))

(:doc +weeks
  (summary "Weeks later.")
  (arg x "A date, a datetime or a moment.")
  (arg n "How many weeks.")
  (returns "x moved by 7 × n calendar days.")
  (raises OverflowException "When the result is outside the calendar.")
  (see -weeks))

(:doc +days
  (summary "Calendar days later.")
  (arg x "A date, a datetime or a moment.")
  (arg n "How many days.")
  (returns "x moved by n days; a moment keeps its local time, which across a change of offset is not 24 hours a day.")
  (raises OverflowException "When the result is outside the calendar.")
  (see -days +hours)
  (example """
    (+days meeting 1)    ;; 2026-10-25T12:00:00+01:00[Europe/Stockholm]
    (+hours meeting 24)  ;; 2026-10-25T11:00:00+01:00[Europe/Stockholm]
    """))

(:doc -years
  (summary "Years earlier.")
  (arg x "A date, a datetime or a moment.")
  (arg n "How many years.")
  (returns "x moved back by n years.")
  (raises OverflowException "When the result is outside the calendar.")
  (see +years))

(:doc -months
  (summary "Months earlier, clamped to the end of the month.")
  (arg x "A date, a datetime or a moment.")
  (arg n "How many months.")
  (returns "x moved back by n months.")
  (raises OverflowException "When the result is outside the calendar.")
  (see +months))

(:doc -weeks
  (summary "Weeks earlier.")
  (arg x "A date, a datetime or a moment.")
  (arg n "How many weeks.")
  (returns "x moved back by 7 × n calendar days.")
  (raises OverflowException "When the result is outside the calendar.")
  (see +weeks))

(:doc -days
  (summary "Calendar days earlier.")
  (arg x "A date, a datetime or a moment.")
  (arg n "How many days.")
  (returns "x moved back by n days.")
  (raises OverflowException "When the result is outside the calendar: @code{(-days (date 1 1 1) 1)} raises.")
  (see +days))

(:doc -date-period
  (summary "A value moved back by calendar amounts.")
  (arg x "A date, a datetime or a moment.")
  (arg p "The period, every amount negated and then applied as +date-period applies it.")
  (returns "x moved back by p.")
  (raises OverflowException "When the result is outside the calendar.")
  (see +date-period -date-period/resolver))

(:doc +hours
  (summary "Hours later.")
  (arg x "A time, a datetime, a moment, an instant or a duration.")
  (arg n "How many hours.")
  (returns "x moved by n hours; a time wraps at midnight.")
  (raises OverflowException "When n hours is outside ±29 227 years, or the result is outside the calendar.")
  (see -hours +duration +days))

(:doc +minutes
  (summary "Minutes later.")
  (arg x "A time, a datetime, a moment, an instant or a duration.")
  (arg n "How many minutes.")
  (returns "x moved by n minutes; a time wraps at midnight.")
  (raises OverflowException "When n minutes is outside ±29 227 years, or the result is outside the calendar.")
  (see -minutes +duration))

(:doc +seconds
  (summary "Seconds later.")
  (arg x "A time, a datetime, a moment, an instant or a duration.")
  (arg n "How many seconds.")
  (returns "x moved by n seconds; a time wraps at midnight.")
  (raises OverflowException "When n seconds is outside ±29 227 years, or the result is outside the calendar.")
  (see -seconds +duration))

(:doc +milliseconds
  (summary "Milliseconds later.")
  (arg x "A time, a datetime, a moment, an instant or a duration.")
  (arg n "How many milliseconds.")
  (returns "x moved by n milliseconds; a time wraps at midnight.")
  (raises OverflowException "When n milliseconds is outside ±29 227 years, or the result is outside the calendar.")
  (see -milliseconds +duration))

(:doc -hours
  (summary "Hours earlier.")
  (arg x "A time, a datetime, a moment, an instant or a duration.")
  (arg n "How many hours.")
  (returns "x moved back by n hours: 01:00 minus three hours is 22:00.")
  (raises OverflowException "When n hours is outside ±29 227 years, or the result is outside the calendar.")
  (see +hours -duration))

(:doc -minutes
  (summary "Minutes earlier.")
  (arg x "A time, a datetime, a moment, an instant or a duration.")
  (arg n "How many minutes.")
  (returns "x moved back by n minutes.")
  (raises OverflowException "When n minutes is outside ±29 227 years, or the result is outside the calendar.")
  (see +minutes))

(:doc -seconds
  (summary "Seconds earlier.")
  (arg x "A time, a datetime, a moment, an instant or a duration.")
  (arg n "How many seconds.")
  (returns "x moved back by n seconds.")
  (raises OverflowException "When n seconds is outside ±29 227 years, or the result is outside the calendar.")
  (see +seconds))

(:doc -milliseconds
  (summary "Milliseconds earlier.")
  (arg x "A time, a datetime, a moment, an instant or a duration.")
  (arg n "How many milliseconds.")
  (returns "x moved back by n milliseconds.")
  (raises OverflowException "When n milliseconds is outside ±29 227 years, or the result is outside the calendar.")
  (see +milliseconds))

(:doc -duration
  (summary "A value moved back by an exact length of time.")
  (arg x "A time, a datetime, a moment, an instant or a duration.")
  (arg d "The duration.")
  (returns "x moved back by d, of x's type.")
  (raises OverflowException "When the result is outside the calendar, or a duration outside ±29 227 years.")
  (see +duration duration-negate))

(:doc +period
  (summary "A value moved by a period: the calendar part, then the duration.")
  (arg x "A datetime or a moment.")
  (arg p "The period.")
  (returns "x moved by p's calendar amounts and then by its duration.")
  (raises OverflowException "When the result is outside the calendar.")
  (see -period period))

(:doc -period
  (summary "A value moved back by a period: the calendar part, then the duration.")
  (arg x "A datetime or a moment.")
  (arg p "The period.")
  (returns "x moved back by p's calendar amounts and then by its duration.")
  (raises OverflowException "When the result is outside the calendar.")
  (see +period))

(:doc duration->hours
  (summary "A duration in whole hours, toward zero.")
  (arg d "The duration.")
  (returns "The whole hours in it."))

(:doc duration->minutes
  (summary "A duration in whole minutes, toward zero.")
  (arg d "The duration.")
  (returns "The whole minutes in it."))

(:doc duration->seconds
  (summary "A duration in whole seconds, toward zero.")
  (arg d "The duration.")
  (returns "The whole seconds in it."))

(:doc duration->milliseconds
  (summary "A duration in whole milliseconds, toward zero.")
  (arg d "The duration.")
  (returns "The whole milliseconds in it."))

(:doc duration-negate
  (summary "A duration with its sign turned.")
  (arg d "The duration.")
  (returns "-d. It never raises, as every duration has a negation."))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Differences
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc years-between
  (summary "Whole years from a to b, by their dates.")
  (arg a "The start.")
  (arg b "The end, of a's type.")
  (returns "Whole months between them divided by 12, toward zero.")
  (raises OverflowException "Near either end of the calendar, as ->date-in-zone-of raises.")
  (see months-between))

(:doc months-between
  (summary "Whole months from a to b, by their dates, as java.time counts them.")
  (arg a "The start.")
  (arg b "The end, of a's type.")
  (returns "The whole months, toward zero.")
  (raises OverflowException "Near either end of the calendar, as ->date-in-zone-of raises.")
  (see days-between years-between)
  (reference """
    A month counts once the day of the month is reached, so
    @code{(months-between 2026-01-31 2026-02-28)} is 0, although
    @code{(+months 2026-01-31 1)} is 2026-02-28. In return, swapping the two
    dates only flips the sign.
    """))

(:doc weeks-between
  (summary "Whole weeks from a to b, by their dates.")
  (arg a "The start.")
  (arg b "The end, of a's type.")
  (returns "The days between them divided by 7, toward zero.")
  (raises OverflowException "Near either end of the calendar, as ->date-in-zone-of raises.")
  (see days-between))

(:doc days-between
  (summary "Calendar days from a to b, by their dates only.")
  (arg a "The start.")
  (arg b "The end, of a's type.")
  (returns "The days between the two dates: 23:00 to 01:00 the next day is 1.")
  (raises OverflowException "Near either end of the calendar, as ->date-in-zone-of raises.")
  (see hours-between months-between ->date-in-zone-of)
  (example """
    (days-between some-date (->date some-moment))   ;; to mix types, say which date
    """)
  (reference """
    The calendar differences read the two dates only, of two values of one
    type (@code{DateDiff}). Between two moments in different zones, the
    second is read in the first one's zone, as java.time does:
    2026-10-25T20:00Z is the 25th in New York and the 26th in Tokyo, and
    those two moments are 0 days apart. So across zones, swapping the
    arguments also swaps the zone the dates are read in. For the time that
    passed, use @code{hours-between}.
    """))

(:doc duration-between
  (summary "The time that passed from a to b.")
  (arg a "The start: an instant or a moment.")
  (arg b "The end: an instant or a moment, not necessarily of a's type.")
  (returns "The duration between their instants, negative when b is before a.")
  (see hours-between days-between)
  (reference """
    Between two things with an instant (@code{HasInstant}): moments,
    instants, or one of each. 2026-10-25 00:00 to 2026-10-26 00:00 is 25
    hours as Stockholm moments, as the clocks went back that night.

    Two @code{DateTime} values have no instant and do not compile. Their
    wall-clock difference, which is not the time that passed across a change
    of offset, is spelled out by reading both as UTC:
    @code{(hours-between (utc-datetime->instant a) (utc-datetime->instant b))}
    is 24.
    """))

(:doc hours-between
  (summary "Whole hours that passed from a to b, toward zero.")
  (arg a "The start: an instant or a moment.")
  (arg b "The end: an instant or a moment.")
  (returns "The whole hours of duration-between.")
  (see duration-between days-between))

(:doc minutes-between
  (summary "Whole minutes that passed from a to b, toward zero.")
  (arg a "The start: an instant or a moment.")
  (arg b "The end: an instant or a moment.")
  (returns "The whole minutes of duration-between.")
  (see duration-between))

(:doc seconds-between
  (summary "Whole seconds that passed from a to b, toward zero.")
  (arg a "The start: an instant or a moment.")
  (arg b "The end: an instant or a moment.")
  (returns "The whole seconds of duration-between.")
  (see duration-between))

(:doc milliseconds-between
  (summary "Whole milliseconds that passed from a to b, toward zero.")
  (arg a "The start: an instant or a moment.")
  (arg b "The end: an instant or a moment.")
  (returns "The whole milliseconds of duration-between.")
  (see duration-between))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Text
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc iso8601->date
  (summary "Reads an ISO 8601 date, 2026-10-25.")
  (arg s "The text.")
  (returns "Some date, or None when s is not a whole, valid date in the extended format.")
  (see text->date))

(:doc iso8601->time
  (summary "Reads an ISO 8601 time of day: 02:30, 02:30:00, 02:30:00.5 or 02:30:00,5.")
  (arg s "The text.")
  (returns "Some time, or None. A fraction has up to seven digits kept; more are read and dropped.")
  (see text->time))

(:doc iso8601->datetime
  (summary "Reads an ISO 8601 date and time, 2026-10-25T02:30:00.")
  (arg s "The text, with an upper case T.")
  (returns "Some datetime, or None.")
  (see text->datetime))

(:doc iso8601->instant
  (summary "Reads an ISO 8601 date and time with an offset, converted to UTC.")
  (arg s "The text: 2026-10-25T01:30:00Z, or any offset, such as +01:00.")
  (returns "Some instant, or None, also when the instant is outside the calendar.")
  (see text->instant))

(:doc iso8601->moment
  (summary "Reads an ISO 8601 date and time with an offset and a zone in brackets.")
  (arg s "The text: 2026-10-25T02:30:00+01:00[Europe/Stockholm].")
  (returns "Some moment, or None.")
  (see text->moment)
  (reference """
    The offset has to be one its zone has at that local time, so
    @code{2026-07-01T12:00:00+01:00[Europe/Stockholm]} is @code{None} (July
    is +02:00), and so is any local time in a gap. A local time in an
    overlap reads at either of its offsets. An id the machine does not know
    is @code{None}.
    """))

(:doc iso8601->duration
  (summary "Reads an ISO 8601 duration: PT26H30M, -PT1.5S.")
  (arg s "The text.")
  (returns "Some duration, or None.")
  (see iso8601->period)
  (reference """
    A duration has no days, since a day is a calendar amount: @code{P1D} is
    a @code{DatePeriod}, and @code{None} here. Only seconds take a fraction.
    """))

(:doc iso8601->date-period
  (summary "Reads an ISO 8601 date period: P1Y2M3W4D, P1M-3D.")
  (arg s "The text.")
  (returns "Some period, or None. Each amount may have a sign of its own.")
  (see iso8601->period))

(:doc iso8601->period
  (summary "Reads an ISO 8601 period with date and time parts: P1DT3H.")
  (arg s "The text.")
  (returns "Some period, or None. A T needs a time part after it.")
  (see iso8601->date-period iso8601->duration))

(:doc instant->http-date
  (summary "Writes an instant as an HTTP date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 01:30:00 GMT.")
  (arg i "The instant.")
  (returns "Its IMF-fixdate (RFC 9110). The fraction of a second is dropped.")
  (see http-date->instant))

(:doc http-date->instant
  (summary "Reads an HTTP date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 01:30:00 GMT.")
  (arg s "An IMF-fixdate (RFC 9110).")
  (returns "Some instant, or None.")
  (see instant->http-date)
  (reference """
    The day name has to be the date's own. The two obsolete forms, RFC 850
    and asctime, are not read.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Text of your own
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc DateNumber
  (summary "The numbers of a date, as a format names them: year, month, day and the others.")
  (case NumYear "year, the year.")
  (case NumMonth "month, 1 to 12.")
  (case NumDay "day, the day of the month.")
  (case NumWeekday "weekday, 1 for Monday to 7 for Sunday.")
  (case NumYday "yday, the day of the year.")
  (case NumWeek "week, the ISO week.")
  (case NumWeekYear "week-year, the year the ISO week is in.")
  (see DatePart date->text)
  (reference """
    Each is written by its tag, alone or with a width: @code{year} and
    @code{(year)} are the same, and @code{(year 4)} fills with zeros to
    four digits.
    """))

(:doc RomanNumber
  (summary "What (roman ...) writes in a format: year, month or day.")
  (case RomanYear "(roman year).")
  (case RomanMonth "(roman month).")
  (case RomanDay "(roman day).")
  (see DatePart)
  (reference """
    No width, as a numeral has no zeros to fill with:
    @code{(roman (year 4))} is a compile error.
    """))

(:doc DatePart
  (summary "A part of a date's format, for date->text and text->date.")
  (case DateNum "A number: year, month, day, weekday, yday, week or week-year.")
  (case DateRoman "(roman year), (roman month) or (roman day).")
  (case MonthName "month-name, from the names table.")
  (case MonthAbbr "month-abbr, from the names table.")
  (case WeekdayName "weekday-name, from the names table.")
  (case WeekdayAbbr "weekday-abbr, from the names table.")
  (case DateText "A string, written as it stands.")
  (case DateChar "A character, written as it stands.")
  (see date->text text->date DateNumber RomanNumber))

(:doc TimePart
  (summary "A part of a time's format, for time->text and text->time.")
  (case TimeHours "hours, 0 to 23.")
  (case TimeHours12 "hours12, 1 to 12: 12 for midnight's and noon's hour.")
  (case TimeMinutes "minutes.")
  (case TimeSeconds "seconds.")
  (case TimeFraction "fraction: bare, ISO 8601's, point included; (fraction n), n digits.")
  (case TimeAmPm "am-pm, the names table's word for the morning or the afternoon.")
  (case TimeText "A string, written as it stands.")
  (case TimeChar "A character, written as it stands.")
  (see time->text text->time))

(:doc DateTimePart
  (summary "A part of a datetime's format: the fields of a date's and of a time's.")
  (case DateTimeDate "A field of a DatePart.")
  (case DateTimeTime "A field of a TimePart.")
  (case DateTimeText "A string, written as it stands.")
  (case DateTimeChar "A character, written as it stands.")
  (see datetime->text text->datetime))

(:doc MomentPart
  (summary "A part of a moment's format: a datetime's fields, offset, zone, unix and unix-ms.")
  (case MomentDateTime "A field of a DateTimePart, of the local time.")
  (case MomentOffset "offset, as ISO 8601 writes it: +01:00.")
  (case MomentZone "zone, the zone's id: Europe/Stockholm.")
  (case MomentUnix "unix, the Unix time in seconds, rounded toward the past.")
  (case MomentUnixMs "unix-ms, the Unix time in milliseconds, rounded toward the past.")
  (case MomentText "A string, written as it stands.")
  (case MomentChar "A character, written as it stands.")
  (see moment->text text->moment))

(:doc InstantPart
  (summary "A part of an instant's format: a datetime's fields in UTC, offset, unix and unix-ms.")
  (case InstantDateTime "A field of a DateTimePart, in UTC.")
  (case InstantOffset "offset, which for an instant is Z.")
  (case InstantUnix "unix, the Unix time in seconds, rounded toward the past.")
  (case InstantUnixMs "unix-ms, the Unix time in milliseconds, rounded toward the past.")
  (case InstantText "A string, written as it stands.")
  (case InstantChar "A character, written as it stands.")
  (see instant->text text->instant)
  (reference """
    An instant has no @code{zone} to write.
    """))

(:doc DateNames
  (summary "The names a format writes and reads: months, weekdays, and the words of am-pm.")
  (field months "Twelve month names, from January.")
  (field months-short "Twelve abbreviated month names, from January.")
  (field weekdays "Seven weekday names, from Monday.")
  (field weekdays-short "Seven abbreviated weekday names, from Monday.")
  (field am "What am-pm writes before noon.")
  (field pm "What am-pm writes from noon.")
  (see english-names swedish-names current-date-names)
  (reference """
    A table of your own for another language is a @code{DateNames} with
    these six fields. One with the wrong number of names raises an
    @code{ArgumentException} when a name is taken from it, in writing or in
    reading.
    """))

(:doc english-names
  (summary "English names: January, Jan, Monday, Mon, AM and PM.")
  (see swedish-names current-date-names))

(:doc swedish-names
  (summary "Swedish names: januari, jan, måndag, mån, fm and em.")
  (see english-names current-date-names))

(:doc current-date-names
  (summary "The names table the writers and readers use when not given #:names.")
  (see english-names swedish-names DateNames)
  (reference """
    A @code{(Param DateNames)}, starting as @code{english-names}.
    """))

(:doc date->text
  (summary "Writes a date in a format of your own.")
  (arg d "The date.")
  (arg parts """The format, a quoted list: @code{'((year 4) "-" (month 2) "-" (day 2))}.""")
  (key #:names "The names month-name and the others write.")
  (returns "The text.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When a part is given more than one width, as @code{(year 4 2)} is, or a name is taken from a table without twelve months or seven weekdays.")
  (see text->date time->text datetime->text moment->text instant->text DatePart)
  (example """
    (date->text d '((year 4) "-" (month 2) "-" (day 2)))            ;; 2026-10-25
    (date->text d '(weekday-name ", " day " " month-name " " year))  ;; Sunday, 25 October 2026
    (date->text d '(day "." (roman month) "." (roman year)))         ;; 25.X.MMXXVI
    """)
  (reference """
    A format is a quoted list: a field's name, a string or a character, each
    written as it stands. A number after a field's name is a width, filled
    with zeros: @code{(month 2)} is @code{03}. Without one, a number takes
    the digits it needs, and @code{day} and @code{(day)} are the same.

    There is one format union and one writer per type, so the writers and
    their formats are:

    @read-table{
    | Writer                  | Format              | Fields |
    |-------------------------+---------------------+--------|
    | @code{date->text}       | @code{DatePart}     | @code{year}, @code{month}, @code{day}, @code{weekday} (1 is Monday), @code{yday}, @code{week}, @code{week-year}; @code{(roman year)}, @code{(roman month)}, @code{(roman day)}; @code{month-name}, @code{month-abbr}, @code{weekday-name}, @code{weekday-abbr} |
    | @code{time->text}       | @code{TimePart}     | @code{hours}, @code{hours12} (12 for midnight's and noon's hour), @code{minutes}, @code{seconds}, @code{fraction}, @code{am-pm} |
    | @code{datetime->text}   | @code{DateTimePart} | both of those |
    | @code{moment->text}     | @code{MomentPart}   | both, @code{offset} (@code{+01:00}), @code{zone} (@code{Europe/Stockholm}), and @code{unix} and @code{unix-ms} |
    | @code{instant->text}    | @code{InstantPart}  | the fields of a @code{DateTimePart}, @code{offset}, @code{unix} and @code{unix-ms} |
    }

    @itemlist{
      @item{@code{(fraction n)} is @code{n} digits of the second's fraction,
        cut rather than rounded, and zeros past the seventh:
        @code{(fraction 9)} is nanoseconds. A bare @code{fraction} is ISO
        8601's, point included: @code{.5}, and nothing at all for a whole
        second, so @code{'((hours 2) ":" (minutes 2) fraction)} reads well
        either way.}
      @item{@code{(roman year)} is @code{MMXXVI}, and 1900 is @code{MCM}.
        Numerals stop at @code{MMMCMXCIX}, so a later year goes on adding
        @code{M}: 4000 is @code{MMMM}. A numeral takes no width.}
      @item{@code{unix} is the Unix time in seconds and @code{unix-ms} in
        milliseconds, both rounded toward the past, as
        @code{instant->unix-seconds} rounds: half a second before 1970 is
        @code{-1} and @code{-500}.}
      @item{A format kept in a variable says which kind it is, since nothing
        else tells the list what it holds:
        @code{(def (: stamp (List DateTimePart)) '(...))}.}
    }

    The mistakes are compile errors, with the list of the fields there are:

    @codeblock[#:lang "bjolang"]|{
    (date->text d '((year 4) (hours 2)))        ;; `hours` is not a tag of the union DatePart …
    (datetime->text dt '((hours 2) offset))     ;; `offset` is not a tag of the union DateTimePart …
    (date->text d '((roman (year 4))))          ;; RomanYear takes no argument …
    (datetime->text some-moment '(year))        ;; a type error: write (->datetime some-moment)
    }|

    A moment's date is written with @code{(date->text (->date m) ...)},
    which says whose date it is. Writing has none of the limits reading has:
    a format that cannot read can still write. Beside @code{year},
    @code{week} is written without a word, so
    @code{'((year 4) "-W" (week 2))} writes 2027-01-01 as @code{2027-W53},
    a week 2027 does not have; write @code{week-year} beside @code{week}.
    """))

(:doc time->text
  (summary "Writes a time of day in a format of your own.")
  (arg t "The time.")
  (arg parts "The format, a list of TimePart.")
  (key #:names "The words am-pm writes.")
  (returns "The text.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When a part is given more than one width.")
  (see text->time date->text TimePart)
  (example """
    (time->text t '(hours12 ":" (minutes 2) " " am-pm))   ;; 1:05 PM
    """)
  (reference """
    The fields are listed under @code{date->text}.
    """))

(:doc datetime->text
  (summary "Writes a date and time in a format of your own.")
  (arg dt "The datetime.")
  (arg parts "The format, a list of DateTimePart: a date's fields and a time's.")
  (key #:names "The names the name fields and am-pm write.")
  (returns "The text.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When a part is given more than one width, or a name is taken from a table without twelve months or seven weekdays.")
  (see text->datetime date->text ->datetime))

(:doc moment->text
  (summary "Writes a moment in a format of your own.")
  (arg m "The moment.")
  (arg parts "The format, a list of MomentPart: its local date and time, offset, zone, unix and unix-ms.")
  (key #:names "The names the name fields and am-pm write.")
  (returns "The text.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When a part is given more than one width, or a name is taken from a table without twelve months or seven weekdays.")
  (see text->moment date->text)
  (example """
    (moment->text m '((year 4) "-" (month 2) "-" (day 2) "T" (hours 2) ":" (minutes 2)
                      ":" (seconds 2) offset))   ;; 2026-10-25T02:30:00+01:00
    """)
  (reference """
    A moment's @code{unix} and @code{unix-ms} are its instant's.
    """))

(:doc instant->text
  (summary "Writes an instant in a format of your own, in UTC.")
  (arg i "The instant.")
  (arg parts "The format, a list of InstantPart: a datetime's fields, offset, unix and unix-ms.")
  (key #:names "The names the name fields and am-pm write.")
  (returns "The text.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When a part is given more than one width, or a name is taken from a table without twelve months or seven weekdays.")
  (see text->instant date->text)
  (reference """
    An instant is written in UTC, and its @code{offset} is @code{Z}, so
    @code{'((year 4) "-" (month 2) "-" (day 2) "T" (hours 2) ":" (minutes 2) ":" (seconds 2) offset)}
    writes what @code{->str} writes for a whole second:
    @code{2026-10-25T01:30:00Z}.
    """))

(:doc text->date
  (summary "Reads a date in a format of your own, the one that wrote it.")
  (arg s "The text.")
  (arg parts "The format, as date->text takes it.")
  (key #:names "The names month-name and the others read.")
  (returns "Some date, or None when the text does not fit the format or names no date.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When the format cannot be read, whatever the text: see below.")
  (see date->text text->time text->datetime text->instant text->moment iso8601->date)
  (example """
    (text->date "2026-10-25" '((year 4) "-" (month 2) "-" (day 2)))   ;; 2026-10-25
    (text->date "Sunday, 25 October 2026"
                '(weekday-name ", " day " " month-name " " year))    ;; 2026-10-25
    (text->date "25.X.MMXXVI" '(day "." (roman month) "." (roman year)))
    (text->date "2026-10" '((year 4) "-" (month 2)))                  ;; 2026-10-01
    (text->date "2026-W43" '((week-year 4) "-W" (week 2)))            ;; 2026-10-19
    """)
  (reference """
    Each writer has a reader, which reads a text with the format that wrote
    it and answers an @code{Option}: @code{text->date}, @code{text->time},
    @code{text->datetime}, @code{text->instant}, @code{text->moment} and
    @code{text->moment/resolver}. Each takes @code{#:names} as the writers
    do. What follows holds for all of them.

    The parts are read in order, and the whole text has to be used. Text
    that does not fit is @code{None}, never an exception.

    @itemlist{
      @item{Text and characters match exactly, case included.}
      @item{A number is ASCII digits only. Without a width it is one digit up
        to the field's most; with @code{(field w)} it is at least @code{w},
        and at most @code{w} or the field's most, whichever is more. The most
        digits are 4 for @code{year} and @code{week-year}, 3 for
        @code{yday}, 1 for @code{weekday}, and 2 for the others. A number
        takes as many digits as there are, up to its most, and never gives
        one back.}
      @item{A name, and @code{am-pm}, matches its table's own spelling, case
        included: @code{oktober}, not @code{Oktober}, and @code{PM}, not
        @code{pm}. When two names match, the longer one is read.}
      @item{A Roman numeral is read only in the form it is written:
        @code{MCM}, not @code{MDCCCC}, and @code{IV}, not @code{IIII}.}
      @item{@code{(fraction n)} is exactly @code{n} digits, and digits past
        the seventh are read and dropped. A bare @code{fraction} is ISO
        8601's: nothing, or a point or a comma and at least one digit.}
      @item{@code{unix} is an optional minus and up to 12 digits, and
        @code{unix-ms} up to 15. A time outside the years 1 to 9999 is
        @code{None}, as @code{unix-seconds->instant} answers.}
      @item{@code{offset} is @code{Z}, or @code{+hh:mm} or @code{-hh:mm} with
        an optional @code{:ss}.}
      @item{@code{zone} is the longest run of the characters an IANA id is
        made of (ASCII letters and digits, @code{/}, @code{_}, @code{-} and
        @code{+}), and an id the machine does not know is @code{None}.}
    }

    @strong{The date.} The fields read make the date:

    @itemlist{
      @item{year, month and day; or year and @code{yday}, the ordinal date
        2026-298; or @code{week-year}, @code{week} and @code{weekday}, the
        week date 2026-W43-7.}
      @item{A field the format leaves out is its first value: no @code{day}
        is the 1st, and no @code{weekday} is Monday. So what HTML's
        @code{<input type="month">} sends, @code{2026-10}, reads with
        @code{'((year 4) "-" (month 2))} as 2026-10-01, and what its
        @code{<input type="week">} sends, @code{2026-W43}, reads with
        @code{'((week-year 4) "-W" (week 2))} as that week's Monday,
        2026-10-19.}
      @item{Every other field read has to be the date's own. A weekday name
        that is not the date's, a @code{week} or @code{yday} beside a
        calendar date that says another, or a Roman year beside a numeric
        one that says another, is @code{None}. So is a value no date has:
        February 30, week 53 of 2027, year 0.}
    }

    @strong{The time.}

    @itemlist{
      @item{@code{hours}, or @code{hours12} and @code{am-pm}, where 12 AM is
        the hour 00 and 12 PM the hour 12. Minutes, seconds and the fraction
        left out are 0.}
      @item{@code{am-pm} beside @code{hours} has to agree with it, and two
        fractions have to agree to the fewer digits of the two.}
      @item{A datetime is a date and a time, and without any time field it is
        midnight.}
    }

    @strong{Formats that cannot be read.} Each raises an
    @code{ArgumentException} when it is used to read, whatever the text,
    with a message that names the part:

    @itemlist{
      @item{@strong{A date without a year.} A date's format needs
        @code{year}, @code{(roman year)} or @code{week-year}:
        @code{'(month "/" day)} cannot say which year.}
      @item{@strong{A field without its own kind of year.} Month, day and
        @code{yday} count within @code{year}, and @code{week} within
        @code{week-year}. @code{'((year 4) "-W" (week 2))} would read
        @code{2026-W43} as nothing, and @code{2026-W01} as January 1, which
        is that week's Thursday; it is refused, and a week is read with
        @code{week-year}. A field of the other kind may stand beside a date
        its own kind makes, and is checked against it:
        @code{'((year 4) "-" (month 2) "-" (day 2) " W" (week 2))} reads.}
      @item{@strong{A time without hours.} A time's format needs
        @code{hours}, or @code{hours12} with @code{am-pm}, and so does a
        datetime's that has minutes, seconds, a fraction or @code{am-pm}.}
      @item{@strong{@code{hours12} without @code{am-pm}}: 1:05 could be
        either.}
      @item{@strong{A moment without a zone.} @code{text->moment}'s format
        needs @code{zone}.}
      @item{@strong{A Unix time beside another field of the instant.}
        @code{unix} or @code{unix-ms} says the whole instant, and a second
        field could say another, so a format that reads one has no other
        field but text and, for a moment, @code{zone}.
        @code{'(unix " " zone)} reads, and @code{'(unix " " (year 4))} and
        @code{'(unix " " unix-ms)} are refused.}
      @item{@strong{A text that reads two ways.} A part that can be of more
        than one length (a number without a width or with a width below its
        most digits, a Roman numeral, a bare fraction, @code{unix},
        @code{zone}, or an @code{offset}, which may have seconds) followed by
        a part that can begin with a character it could also take.
        @code{'(year month day)} reads @code{2026110} as January 10 or as
        November 0, so it is refused. @code{'((year 4) (month 2) (day 2))}
        reads, and so does @code{'(year "-" month "-" day)}. A bare fraction
        takes a point and the digits after it, a zone the characters of an
        id, and an offset a colon, so none of those may follow them:
        @code{'(offset "[" zone "]")} reads.}
      @item{@strong{A names table without twelve months or seven weekdays},
        when the format reads a name from it, as when it writes one.}
    }

    Writing has none of these limits: a format that cannot read can still
    write.

    @strong{Round trips.} What a writer writes with a format that can be
    read, the reader of the same type reads back with that format. It gives
    back the value, or what the format keeps of it: without seconds, the
    minute; without a day, the 1st; with @code{(fraction 3)}, the
    millisecond; with @code{unix}, the second, rounded toward the past. The
    tests write and read thousands of values of every type so, across the
    whole range and in zones with offsets such as Kathmandu's +05:45 and
    Chatham's +12:45.

    @strong{Cost.} A reader allocates nothing but a moment's zone id. It
    walks the format part by part and checks it on every read, so it costs
    about ten times what the @code{iso8601->} readers do, which stay the
    fast path for ISO 8601 text.
    """))

(:doc text->time
  (summary "Reads a time of day in a format of your own.")
  (arg s "The text.")
  (arg parts "The format, as time->text takes it.")
  (key #:names "The words am-pm reads.")
  (returns "Some time, or None.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When the format cannot be read: without hours, or with hours12 and no am-pm.")
  (see time->text text->date)
  (example """
    (text->time "1:05 PM" '(hours12 ":" (minutes 2) " " am-pm))   ;; 13:05:00
    """)
  (reference """
    The rules are under @code{text->date}.
    """))

(:doc text->datetime
  (summary "Reads a date and time in a format of your own.")
  (arg s "The text.")
  (arg parts "The format, as datetime->text takes it.")
  (key #:names "The names the name fields and am-pm read.")
  (returns "Some datetime, or None. Without any time field it is midnight.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When the format cannot be read, as text->date and text->time say.")
  (see datetime->text text->date)
  (example """
    (text->datetime "2026-10-25 02:30"
                    '((year 4) "-" (month 2) "-" (day 2) " " (hours 2) ":" (minutes 2)))
    """))

(:doc text->instant
  (summary "Reads an instant in a format of your own: UTC's fields, unless the format has an offset.")
  (arg s "The text.")
  (arg parts "The format, as instant->text takes it.")
  (key #:names "The names the name fields and am-pm read.")
  (returns "Some instant, or None.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When the format cannot be read, as text->date says.")
  (see instant->text text->moment instant->moment)
  (example """
    (text->instant "2026-10-25T02:30:00+01:00"
                   '((year 4) "-" (month 2) "-" (day 2) "T" (hours 2) ":" (minutes 2) ":"
                     (seconds 2) offset))                             ;; 2026-10-25T01:30:00Z
    (text->instant "@1792891800" '("@" unix))                         ;; 2026-10-25T01:30:00Z
    """)
  (reference """
    An instant is its Unix time, or a date and a time. Those are UTC's, as
    @code{instant->text} writes them, unless the format has an
    @code{offset}, and then they are the local time at that offset: the
    inverse of the writer, and no guess at a zone.

    A text with an offset and no zone, as RFC 3339 writes, is read with
    @code{text->instant} and put in a zone with @code{instant->moment}: two
    steps, and no zone is guessed.
    """))

(:doc text->moment
  (summary "Reads a moment in a format of your own, which names its zone.")
  (arg s "The text.")
  (arg parts "The format, as moment->text takes it. It needs zone.")
  (key #:names "The names the name fields and am-pm read.")
  (returns "Some moment, or None.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When the format has no zone, or cannot be read as text->date says.")
  (see moment->text text->moment/resolver text->instant iso8601->moment)
  (example """
    (text->moment "2026-10-25 12:30 Europe/Stockholm"
                  '((year 4) "-" (month 2) "-" (day 2) " " (hours 2) ":" (minutes 2) " " zone))
    """)
  (reference """
    A moment is in the zone it reads, and a Unix time is put in that zone. A
    local time with an @code{offset} has to be at an offset the zone has
    then, as @code{iso8601->moment} demands: in Stockholm, 2026-07-01
    12:00+01:00 is @code{None}, as July is +02:00, and 2026-10-25 02:30
    reads at +02:00 and at +01:00, as the clocks go back that night. A local
    time without an offset is @code{None} in a gap or an overlap, as
    @code{datetime->moment/opt} answers.

    A local time whose instant is outside the range, such as 0001-01-01
    00:00 in Stockholm, is @code{None}, and so is a Unix time whose local
    time is, such as the first second in New York.

    It never guesses a zone: a text with an offset and no zone is an
    instant, read with @code{text->instant}.
    """))

(:doc text->moment/resolver
  (summary "Reads a moment in a format of your own, resolving a local time in a gap or an overlap.")
  (arg s "The text.")
  (arg parts "The format, as moment->text takes it. It needs zone.")
  (arg r "What to do with a local time without an offset in a gap or an overlap.")
  (key #:names "The names the name fields and am-pm read.")
  (returns "Some moment, or None.")
  (raises ArgumentException "When the format has no zone, or cannot be read as text->date says.")
  (see text->moment datetime->moment)
  (reference """
    As @code{text->moment}, except that a local time without an offset is
    found as @code{datetime->moment} finds it. Where the local time and its
    instant cannot both be in the calendar, it is @code{None}.
    """))
